SQL syntax is nothing more than a precise, machine-readable declaration that must be parsed into an internal tree the database engine can execute.
The engine does not read English. It reads a strict sequence:
Command verb → Target object → Properties of that object.
Every DDL command follows this exact three-part skeleton.
The punctuation (parentheses, commas, semicolons) exists only to mark boundaries so the parser can split the declaration cleanly.
No magic. No poetry. Just a contract written in a language the machine can compile into actions on the system catalog.
That is the entire, provable foundation. Nothing else remains.
Step 3: Rebuild the Fundamental Syntax from Only What Remains
We now rebuild the syntax for each command using only the three-part skeleton above. These are the minimal forms that every relational engine must understand.
1. CREATE → “Declare a new contract”
Fundamental syntax skeleton:
CREATE TABLE table_name ( column_definitions ) ;

Verb = CREATE TABLE
Target object = table_name
Properties = everything inside the parentheses (each column’s name + type + constraints)

This is the only required pattern. Everything else (constraints, indexes, etc.) is just extra detail inside the properties block.
2. ALTER → “Change an existing contract”
Fundamental syntax skeleton:
ALTER TABLE table_name action ;

Verb = ALTER TABLE
Target object = table_name
Properties = action (the single thing you want to change)

The action can be ADD column, DROP column, MODIFY column, etc. — but the engine only cares that you name the table and then say exactly one change.
3. DROP → “Erase the entire contract”
Fundamental syntax skeleton:
DROP TABLE table_name ;

Verb = DROP TABLE
Target object = table_name
Properties = none (nothing left to declare)

That’s it. The engine needs only the name; everything else is destroyed.
4. TRUNCATE → “Reset the contract to empty”
Fundamental syntax skeleton:
TRUNCATE TABLE table_name ;

Verb = TRUNCATE TABLE
Target object = table_name
Properties = none

Again, no extra clauses needed in the minimal form. The engine already knows what “reset to empty” means for this table’s contract.
The Universal Three-Part Pattern (What Remains After Stripping Everything Else)
Every DDL command reduces to exactly this provable template:
VERB OBJECT_NAME ( optional_properties ) ;

VERB is always one of: CREATE TABLE, ALTER TABLE, DROP TABLE, TRUNCATE TABLE
OBJECT_NAME is always the table you are talking about
( optional_properties ) is present only when you are defining or changing something (CREATE and ALTER)
The semicolon ; is the universal “I am finished — execute now” signa
